The pre-flight question that shaped everything: SPEC.md 5.6 deliberately does
NOT specify the manifest key set - it fixes only the table shape, and wax-core
exposes manifest() as an opaque map. So this was an application-level change,
not format-visible. No SPEC bump, no conformance-corpus change, no bytes moved.

Legacy proof used REAL artifacts, not fixtures written for the test: the two
legacy-tier-*.wax packs were built by the previous builder before any code
changed, so they carry a genuine min_hw_tier row.

The finding that matters most, and it is unresolved by design:
mapping a legacy pi_5 LOSES a guarantee. The table Directive 02 deleted gave
pi_5 4 GB / 64 GB; contract 2.1 now puts Pi 4 and Pi 5 in one class at
2 GB / 32 GB. A legacy pi_5 pack therefore resolves BELOW the floor it was
built against, so a 2 GB box would be judged able to run it. No current
document states the right resource point, and inventing one is exactly what
the contract exists to prevent - so inspect reports the loss, a test pins that
it is reported, and contract 11 carries it as an open item the contract owns.
Do not quietly close this by picking 4 GB.

